{"meta":{"course":"cat100-supplement","chapter":"05","section":"04","chapter_title":"AI-Enhanced Presentation Development","title":"AI Narration Pathways","description":"CAT 100 textbook section connecting ai narration pathways to AI critique log showing accepted, modified, and rejected suggestions, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","learning_objectives":["Analyze how the AI critique log showing accepted, modified, and rejected suggestions shows a defensible decision in CAT 100.","Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.","Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.","Use feedback, support requests, and revision notes to improve the artifact before sharing it."],"concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_ai_enhanced_presentation_development","cat100_supplement_ai_narration_pathways","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":4,"top_tier_hardened":false,"prereq_section_ids":["cat100-supplement/05/03"],"practice_ids":["cat100_supplement_5_4_ai_narration_pathways_artifact_signal","cat100_supplement_5_4_ai_narration_pathways_annotation_signal","cat100_supplement_5_4_ai_narration_pathways_ai_boundary","cat100_supplement_5_4_ai_narration_pathways_calibration"]},"content":"# AI Narration Pathways\n\n![AI Narration Pathways textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat100-supplement/05-04-ai-narration-pathways.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nA narrated slide gives the audience two channels at once: the words on screen and the voice in their ears. When those channels say the same thing, you waste half the bandwidth and you trigger the redundancy effect, where reading and listening to identical text actually makes comprehension worse, not better. The voice should add what the slide cannot show: the cause behind a number, the example behind a claim, the recommendation behind the chart.\n\nThis section also covers the practical pathways for getting narration onto a deck, from recording your own voice to generating it with a text-to-speech tool, and the obligations that come with each. The artifact is an AI critique log of the narration choices you accepted, modified, and rejected, including any AI-voice errors you caught and any disclosure the assignment requires.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Analyze how the AI critique log showing accepted, modified, and rejected suggestions shows a defensible decision in CAT 100.\n- Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.\n- Use feedback, support requests, and revision notes to improve the artifact before sharing it.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA student records narration by reading each slide aloud, word for word. The result feels redundant and slightly insulting, because the audience can read faster than he can speak and they finish each bullet before he does. A classmate records narration that never repeats a bullet; instead she says why the number happened and what she recommends. Same slides, same length, completely different value. The voice that adds reasoning is worth listening to; the voice that reads the screen is noise the audience would mute.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nNarration is a second information channel, and its job is to be **complementary**, not redundant. The slide shows the *what* (the chart, the headline, the bullet); the voice supplies the *why* and the *so what* that the slide leaves implicit. This is the multimedia principle in practice: words and visuals teach more together when each carries different, mutually supporting information, and they teach less when the audio merely recites the on-screen text. Both effects are documented in multimedia-learning research: the redundancy principle (Kalyuga & Sweller, 2014) and a direct empirical test showing that on-screen text duplicating narration depresses learning (Chan et al., 2020).\n\nA useful test is the **delete-the-overlap test**. Draft the script, then strike every sentence that only repeats visible text. What remains should be the cause, the example, the caveat, or the recommendation. If almost nothing remains, the narration was redundant and the slide did not need a voice at all.\n\nThe pathway you choose to produce the narration carries its own obligations:\n\n| Pathway | Strength | Obligation |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Record your own voice | Authentic, easy to add emphasis | Proofread audio; re-record stumbles |\n| AI text-to-speech | Fast, consistent, easy to revise | Check pronunciation; disclose AI voice where required |\n| AI-drafted script you read aloud | Fast first draft | Edit for accuracy and your own claims; you own every word |\n\nAs of 2026, current text-to-speech options include ElevenLabs (Eleven v3), the built-in AI narration in Microsoft 365 via Copilot and Clipchamp (which can voice your speaker notes), and tools like Murf; tool names and voice quality churn fast, but the obligations (proofread by ear, caption or transcript, disclose, own every word) are tool-independent.\n\nWhichever pathway you choose, narration adds an accessibility obligation: every narrated slide needs a caption or transcript. WCAG 2.2 requires a text alternative for prerecorded audio (SC 1.2.1, Level A, satisfied by a transcript) and synchronized captions when the narration plays inside a video (SC 1.2.2, Level A). With AI text-to-speech the transcript is essentially free, because it is the script you already wrote as editable text; recorded own-voice narration needs captions or a transcript added afterward. Your critique log should record that a caption or transcript exists for each narrated slide.\n\nAI voices mispronounce domain terms with total confidence (\"cache\" read as \"kashay\"), so an AI narration pathway adds a proofreading-by-ear step that a typed slide never needed. Disclosure is not optional decoration; where the assignment or audience expects to know a voice is synthetic, hiding it is a citizenship failure. Disclosure expectations for AI use are still evolving and vary by instructor, so check your course or institution policy; a workable model as of 2026 is a one-line note naming the tool and how you used it.\n\n## Worked Example\n\nGoal: write narration for a chart slide so the audio adds meaning instead of reading the screen.\n\nOn-screen text: a bar chart titled **\"Signups doubled after launch.\"**\n\nWeak narration (reads the slide):\n\n> \"Signups doubled after launch. As you can see, signups doubled after launch.\"\n\nStronger narration (adds the cause and the so-what):\n\n> \"The jump you see came from the referral feature we shipped in week two. That single change drove most of the doubling, which is why I recommend funding referrals next quarter.\"\n\nSteps:\n\n1. Draft the script with AI, then read it aloud against the slide.\n2. Delete any line that repeats visible text.\n3. Add the reason, an example, or the recommendation the chart cannot show.\n4. Proofread the audio: the AI voice said \"kashay\" for \"cache,\" so re-record that term and disclose AI narration where required.\n\nThe strong version never says \"doubled\" again, because the chart already said it. The voice spends its entire budget on the two things the chart cannot show: what caused the jump and what to do about it. That is complementary narration, and the mispronunciation catch in step 4 is exactly the kind of AI-pathway check your log should record.\n\nWork one narration decision at a time, predicting each move before you reveal it:\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Turn a slide-reading script into complementary narration\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"Your chart slide says \\\"Signups doubled after launch\\\" and your draft narration says \\\"Signups doubled after launch.\\\" What is the first move?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Record it as is; repeating the headline reinforces it\",\"Run the delete-the-overlap test and strike the line that only repeats visible text\",\"Add an animation so the line feels less repetitive\",\"Make the voice louder on that line\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Strike it. The chart already states that signups doubled, so reading it aloud triggers the redundancy effect. The narration budget should go to what the slide cannot show.\"},{\"prompt\":\"You deleted the redundant line. What goes in its place?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"A restatement of the headline in different words\",\"The cause of the jump and the recommendation the chart cannot show\",\"A summary of the whole deck\",\"Nothing; leave the slide silent\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Add the why and the so-what: \\\"The jump came from the week-two referral feature, which is why I recommend funding referrals next quarter.\\\" That is complementary, not redundant.\"},{\"reveal\":\"Generate or record the audio, then proofread by ear. The AI voice read \\\"cache\\\" as \\\"kashay,\\\" so re-record that term and add the required AI-narration disclosure. The log records the redundancy cut, the pronunciation catch, and the disclosure.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\n<inline-check question=\"A student records narration that reads each bullet aloud word for word, reasoning that it is thorough. Why does this lower comprehension?\" explanation=\"Identical audio and on-screen text compete for the same working memory; that is the redundancy effect (Kalyuga & Sweller, 2014; Chan et al., 2020). Comprehension improves only when the voice carries different, complementary information from the slide.\" options='[{\"text\":\"Reading every bullet is thorough and aids memory\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Identical audio and text compete for working memory (the redundancy effect)\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"The voice is simply too quiet to follow\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"The slide has too few bullets to read aloud\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nMake the voice complementary to the slide, not redundant with it, and own every word the audience hears regardless of how it was produced. If the narration only reads the screen, cut it.\n\nCheck a narration pass against four questions:\n\n1. **Purpose check:** After the audio, can the audience act, knowing the why and the so-what, not just the on-screen what?\n2. **Evidence check:** Which sentences add cause, example, or recommendation, and which only repeat visible text?\n3. **Revision check:** What redundant line did you cut, and what AI-voice error or disclosure did you handle?\n4. **Accessibility check:** Does every narrated slide have a caption or transcript? For text-to-speech, the script is the transcript; for your own recorded voice, add one afterward.\n\nIf you removed nothing in the delete-the-overlap test, the narration was redundant.\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe first misreading is that reading the slide aloud is \"safe\" or \"thorough.\" It is the redundancy effect in action: identical audio and text compete for the same working memory and lower comprehension. Thoroughness on screen plus reasoning in the voice is the goal, not the same words twice.\n\nThe second is treating an AI-generated voice as finished once it sounds smooth. Smooth and correct are different; a confident mispronunciation of a key term undermines credibility more than an obvious robotic tone would.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nApply complementary narration to a slide with no chart, just a single claim: \"We should move the deadline to Friday.\" On screen, that is the whole message. The voice cannot repeat it, so it must supply the support the slide omits: \"We moved it because the data load now finishes Thursday night, so a Friday deadline gives the team a full review day instead of zero.\" The audio carried the reasoning; the slide carried the decision.\n\nNow suppose you generate that narration with text-to-speech. You run the audio once with your eyes closed and catch that the tool stressed the wrong word, flattening \"Friday\" into the middle of the sentence so the date got lost. You re-prompt for emphasis or re-record that clause, then add the required disclosure that the narration is AI-generated. The script was complementary; the pathway forced a listen-test and a disclosure that a silent slide never would.\n\nOrder the narration pass so each step depends on the one before it:\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"order\" items='[\"Draft the narration script (your own words or an AI draft you own)\", \"Read it aloud against the slide and run the delete-the-overlap test\", \"Cut every line that only repeats visible on-screen text\", \"Add the cause, example, or recommendation the slide cannot show\", \"Proofread the audio by ear for mispronounced terms\", \"Disclose AI narration where the audience expects to know\"]'></sequence-builder>\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nAs you read, keep a two-column note: on the left, each narration sentence; on the right, whether it repeats the slide or adds cause, example, or recommendation. Flag every redundant line and every term the AI voice could mispronounce. By the end of 05.04, your note should show that the voice earns its place.\n\nA strong reader of this section can answer four questions:\n\n1. What does the voice add that the slide cannot show?\n2. Which sentences are redundant with on-screen text?\n3. Which pathway produced the audio, and what did that obligate you to check?\n4. Where is AI narration disclosed, if the audience expects to know?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nThe critique log is strong when a reviewer can see which narration lines you cut for redundancy, which you added for reasoning, and which AI-voice errors you caught and fixed. Treat a smooth-sounding take as the starting point, not the finish line.\n\nA useful entry would sound like this: \"I cut the line that reread 'signups doubled' and replaced it with the cause (the week-two referral feature) and the recommendation (fund referrals). I accepted the AI voice for speed but rejected its first render because it pronounced 'cache' as 'kashay,' re-recorded that term, and added the required AI-narration disclosure on the closing slide: 'Narration generated with ElevenLabs and edited by the author.'\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the same content to a live, in-person talk with no recorded audio at all. The complementary-narration rule transfers directly: you still do not read your slides, you still speak the cause and the recommendation, and the slide still carries only the what. What drops away is the AI-pathway machinery, the pronunciation check and the disclosure, because there is no synthetic voice. If you can deliver the live version without reading a single bullet, your narration was complementary by design, not by accident.\n\n## Concept Map\n\nConnect the ideas this section builds on. See how making the voice complementary feeds evidence-based revision of the narration log:\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat100_supplement_ai_narration_pathways\", \"cat100_supplement_ai_enhanced_presentation_development\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the AI critique log showing accepted, modified, and rejected suggestions packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat100-supplement/05-04-ai-narration-pathways-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"AI critique log showing accepted, modified, and rejected suggestions\" course=\"CAT 100\" section=\"05.04\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation of complementary narration and the redundancy effect.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare narration that reads the slide with narration that adds the cause and recommendation.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the delete-the-overlap test to a different narrated slide.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where an AI-voice error or disclosure changed your narration plan.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the AI critique log showing accepted, modified, and rejected suggestions for CAT 100 05.04. A peer comment points to a transfer move issue connected to [ISTE digital citizenship](https://iste.org/standards/students). Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"What best shows transfer rather than completion?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The learner explains how the same decision rule changes under a new audience or constraint.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"1\" conceptid=\"cat100_supplement_ai_narration_pathways\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, confirm the narration adds reasoning the slide cannot show, repeats no on-screen text, and that your log records any AI-voice error you fixed and any required disclosure.\n\n## References\n\n- [NACE career readiness](https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined)\n- [ISTE digital citizenship](https://iste.org/standards/students)\n- data-ink and visual evidence. https://www.edwardtufte.com/tufte/books_vdqi\n- [WCAG accessibility](https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/)\n- [Kalyuga & Sweller (2014), The redundancy principle in multimedia learning. In R. E. Mayer (Ed.), The Cambridge Handbook of Multimedia Learning (2nd ed.)](https://doi.org/10.1017/cbo9781139547369.013)\n- [Chan et al. (2020), Effect of on-screen text on multimedia learning with native and foreign-accented narration. Learning and Instruction, 70](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.learninstruc.2020.101305)\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"cat100_supplement_5_4_ai_narration_pathways_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_ai_narration_pathways","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_ai_narration_pathways","cat100_supplement_ai_enhanced_presentation_development"],"stem":"A slide shows a bar chart titled 'Signups doubled after launch.' Which narration script adds value instead of reading the slide?","options":["'Signups doubled after launch. This slide shows signups doubled after launch.'","'The jump you see came from the referral feature we shipped in week two, which is why I recommend funding it next quarter.'","'Here is a chart. It has bars. The bars go up.'","'As you can see on the slide, signups doubled after launch.'"],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Strong narration supplies the cause and the recommendation the chart cannot show, rather than repeating the headline.","difficulty":"easy","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_5_4_ai_narration_pathways_narration_repeats_slide"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_5_4_ai_narration_pathways_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"evidence_based_revision","concept_ids":["evidence_based_revision"],"stem":"Which note gives BigAL a precise narration problem to solve?","options":["My audio is fine.","The AI voice says 'cache' as 'kashay', and my narration just rereads slide 3's bullets. How do I fix pronunciation and add the missing reasoning?","Make my voice better.","I recorded something."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The note names two concrete faults (mispronunciation and slide-reading) so feedback can target both.","difficulty":"medium","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_5_4_ai_narration_pathways_voice_clone_no_disclosure"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_5_4_ai_narration_pathways_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ai_supported_learning","concept_ids":["ai_supported_learning"],"stem":"What is a defensible way to use an AI voice or script for narration?","options":["Generate a synthetic voiceover and submit it without listening to it.","Use an AI script as a first draft, edit it for accuracy and timing, and disclose AI narration where the assignment requires it.","Let AI invent statistics for the narration to sound confident.","Use AI narration only to make the deck longer."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"You keep responsibility for accuracy, timing, and disclosure; the AI output is an editable draft.","difficulty":"hard","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_5_4_ai_narration_pathways_autoscript_as_final"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_5_4_ai_narration_pathways_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"evidence_based_revision","concept_ids":["evidence_based_revision"],"stem":"A reviewer says your narration 'just reads the slides.' Which revision is the real fix?","options":["Increase the audio volume.","Rewrite each line to add the reason, example, or implication the slide text does not state.","Add background music.","Speed up the playback."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Narration earns its place by adding meaning beyond the visible text, not by being louder or faster.","difficulty":"medium"}]},"misconceptions":[{"id":"cat100_supplement_5_4_ai_narration_pathways_narration_repeats_slide","pattern":"narration_reads_the_slide","description":"Writing narration that reads the on-screen text word for word","trigger":"learner records audio that repeats the bullet points verbatim","feedback":"Narration should add what the slide cannot show: the reason, the example, the so-what. Do not read the bullets.","rail_action":"represent"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_5_4_ai_narration_pathways_voice_clone_no_disclosure","pattern":"ai_voice_without_disclosure","description":"Using an AI-generated voice without disclosing it or checking its accuracy","trigger":"learner uses a synthetic voice that mispronounces a key term and adds no note that it is AI","feedback":"Disclose AI narration where required and proofrerad the audio for wrong pronunciations and inserted errors.","rail_action":"explain"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_5_4_ai_narration_pathways_autoscript_as_final","pattern":"ai_script_as_final_draft","description":"Treating an AI-drafted narration script as a finished script","trigger":"learner pastes the model output as the final voiceover with no edits","feedback":"Treat the AI script as a first draft: cut filler, fix claims, and match it to your slide timing.","rail_action":"ask"}],"content_version":{"content_version":"3521a2d22413a219ea6018ed740875403991df7b107d93251cfeadef15354d9f","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}